{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>For Pharaoh's horse came with his chariots and with his horsemen.</b> Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] (on Exodus 15:19) said that this too is part of the song, for they would sing and say: <b>For Pharaoh's horse came with his chariots and with his horsemen into the sea, and He brought back upon them the waters of the sea</b> — while the children of Israel were walking on dry ground in the midst of the sea. And this is a miracle within a miracle, yet it is not in the language of song and prophecy. But its meaning is: \"Then Moses sang\" — for Pharaoh's horse came on that very day, immediately, not on the following day or afterward. Or the meaning of \"then Moses sang\" is: when Pharaoh's horse came into the sea and HaShem brought back upon them the waters of the sea while the children of Israel were walking on dry ground within it — to declare that it was while walking within it on dry ground that they said the song. And he said further: for it was then that Miriam the prophetess took the timbrel in her hand and she responded to them with the first verse of the song, that they should respond thus after Moses and Israel:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The phrase rendered 'and He brought back upon them' follows the verse's וַיָּשֶׁב עֲלֵיהֶם; later in the comment Ramban shifts to וְהֵשִׁיב (causative), reflected as 'brought back' throughout."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:16:56.731767935Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}